    41	## Foundation decisions (Sep 25–27, 2026)
    42	
    43	These come from the foundation plan (`~/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/clayhub-members-foundation.html`), where each has a D-number. That plan went through four independent review rounds.
    44	
    45	| # | Plan | Date | Decision | Why |
    46	|---|---|---|---|---|
    47	| 30 | D0 | Sep 26 | **A separate Firebase project, `my-clay-hub`, only for this app.** There are exactly two projects: `tinker-hq-apps` for every staff app, and `my-clay-hub` for this one. | Members are the first people outside staff to sign in. Keeping their accounts out of `tinker-hq-apps` means no internal rule can ever trust them by mistake. |
    48	| 31 | D1 | Sep 27 | Staff screens are a **`/staff` section of My Clay Hub**, opened from a Tinker HQ tile. Staff sign in with their Tinker HQ login, which a server function exchanges for a My Clay Hub session (`staff_{uid}`). Staff rules check the staff roster live on every request. **To remove someone's staff access, untick them (or make them inactive) in Manage Team.** Disabling their login alone doesn't do it. | Same login and roles as today. Access changes take effect within a minute. |
    49	| 32 | D2 | Sep 27 | Server code in two codebases, `clayhub-link` (in studio-hub, the link only) and `my-clay-hub/functions`. Both deploy only through a functions-aware guard. | The link has to run where Membership Manager's data lives. Everything else stays in this app's own project. |
    50	| 33 | D3 | Sep 27 | **Member sign-in is gated by a claim.** After a magic-link or Google sign-in, `claimMembership` finds exactly one live member with that verified email and a sign-in-eligible status, then sets the claim. Every member rule also checks the member's **live** status. No paid "blocking function". | Only real members get in. A pause or offboarding takes effect immediately, not at the next token refresh. |
    51	| 34 | D4 | Sep 27 | **A member's status is worked out by `deriveStatus`** from the dates, with Membership Manager's `stage` as a backstop. The rules are in a fixed order (DATA-MODEL.md), and the result is one of: active, paused, review, offboarded, not_yet, removed. Specific gaps give `review` (booking locked, feed still visible, staff flagged) instead of a lockout: a missing or unknown stage, a malformed last day, a pause with a missing or malformed date, `offboarding` with no last day, or `paused` with no pause dates. Every rule is in DATA-MODEL.md. The last access day itself still has access. | `stage` is only brought up to date when someone opens Membership Manager. Dates are the truth. |
    52	| 35 | D4a | Sep 27 | **A pause's end date is the last paused day.** Jan 2 – Feb 1 means back on Feb 2 ("Booking opens back up on Feb 2"). | Christie: "the day they can RETURN to the studio is Feb 2." |
    53	| 36 | D5 | Sep 27 | Add an accurate **`memberSince`** to Membership Manager. Christie fills in missing phones. Member-since shows on Profile and in the Reports member panel. | There's no reliable join date today. |
    54	| 37 | D6 | Sep 27 | **Nothing ever writes back to Membership Manager.** | Membership Manager re-saves whole records and would erase anything else. Any change to this would be a new, reviewed decision. |
    55	| 38 | D7 | Sep 27 | Plain collection names in `my-clay-hub` (`members`, `bookings`, `settings`, …). | The project holds only this app. |
    56	| 39 | D8 | Sep 27 | The front-door tablet signs in as its own `my-clay-hub` account (`kiosk:true`). It has no connection to the timeclock kiosks. | The timeclock kiosk can read staff records. This one mustn't. |
    57	| 40 | D9 | Sep 27 | A staff member who is also a Clay Hub member has two identities: a member login and a staff session. | They never mix. Regular staff only get the staff side if their box is ticked. |
    58	| 41 | D10 | Sep 27 | Clay Hub Booking's leftover data is sealed by removing its rules, snapshotted, then deleted by Christie. Only Booking-only login accounts are disabled. | It was never deployed, and it had a security hole. |
    59	| 42 | D11 | Sep 27 | **Permissions are narrow and one way:** dedicated service accounts; the link can read `tinker-hq-apps` but not write it; it can only *invoke* one private function in `my-clay-hub`; the ingest function can't delete. The full inventory is in the foundation plan. | Server code ignores Firestore rules, so it has to be contained by permissions instead. |
    60	| 43 | D12 | Sep 27 | The app is named **My Clay Hub**. Project ID `my-clay-hub` (fallbacks `myclayhub`, `my-clay-hub-app`). Manage Team key `my-clay-hub`. Repo `Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub`. | Christie's choice. |
    61	| 44 | D13 | Sep 27 | `my-clay-hub` backups are **server-side only**: point-in-time recovery, delete protection, a managed daily backup, and the vault export. It isn't added to `backup.js`. | `backup.js` depends on Christie's personal login. |
    62	| 45 | D14 | Sep 27 | **Keep the kiosk name list** (amends #9): members whose booking is live now (15 minutes before start through the end), grouped as "Here now" and "Arriving soon". First names, with a last initial only when two first names match (and the first two letters of the last name when the initials match too). No area. Display-only. | Christie: it's only seen inside the studio, and it builds community by matching names to faces. |
    63	| 46 | D15 | Sep 27 | **Staff no-show alerts in Phase 1**, including phone push for the two staff. They go out at last call (+20 min), for no-shows only, with "They're here: check in" or "Not here". | So Maryssa can check whether someone is actually there before their hours are released. |
    64	| 47 | D16 | Sep 27 | **"Start now"** (amends #16): checking in more than 15 minutes early offers "Start now, same length" or "Start now, keep my end time". The second one counts toward the cap. | Arriving early should be easy. |
    65	| 48 | D17 | Sep 27 | **15-minute times.** Start time is a dropdown. "How long" chips show the length and the end time ("3h / 9:00"), plus an "Other end time" option. The meter shows quarter hours. Hours returned on early check-out are rounded **up** to the next 15 minutes. | Members have always had 15-minute times in Skedda. Rounding up favours the member. |
    66	| 49 | D18 | Sep 27 | Booking opens **14 days** ahead (a setting). | Close to what members have now in Skedda, and still simple. |
    67	| 50 | D19 | Sep 27 | Staff with the `my-clay-hub` box ticked (Maryssa, the studio tech) get the Today view, check-in for others, posts, changes, events, **reports and CSVs**. **Settings** are for managers and admins only. | Maryssa runs reports herself. Settings change the rules for everyone. |
    68	| 51 | D20 | Sep 27 | **The Clay Hub brand**: the bright Clay Hub palette plus Tinker teal, Jost, the logo SVGs, flat-bottomed clay blobs, and pot icons for events. Readability comes first. See DESIGN.md. | Christie's brand sheet. Teal ties it to Tinker. |
    69	| 52 | D21 | Sep 27 | Every member gets a **permanent `memberId`** in Membership Manager, plus a "Change email" action that keeps it. The change is **one atomic batch**: the new doc (same `memberId`, live) and the old doc marked `retired: true`. It **refuses** if a live doc already exists for the new email. Doc IDs stay as they are. | Email-based IDs collide, and an email change used to leave a stale second record. |
    70	| 53 | D22 | Sep 27 | **The one-way link, "re-read, then send a snapshot"** (supersedes #23's "no copy"). When a member record changes, the link re-reads that member's current truth and sends a complete snapshot, stamped with when it was read. The newest snapshot always wins. Only name, email, status dates, member-since and `phoneLast4` are copied. | Correct even when changes arrive late, twice or out of order, and through deletes and email changes. |
    71	| 54 | D23 | Sep 27 | 2nd-generation Cloud Functions. Database location, trigger location and function region recorded separately. Retries explicitly on. Explicit `maxInstances`, one Admin app per instance, and an Artifact Registry cleanup policy. | 1st gen can't be upgraded in place later. Retries are off by default. |
    72	| 55 | — | Sep 27 | **No recurring bookings** (for example "every Tuesday 6–9") in My Clay Hub for now. Members book each session individually, within the 14-day window. | Christie: "let's start simpler without it." The old Clay Hub Booking notes said members used recurring bookings in Skedda, so this is a deliberate choice, not an oversight. It can be revisited later as its own decision. |
    73	| 56 | C6 | Sep 28 | **Every booking belongs wholly to its start date's week.** A booking is `[start, end)`; no booked minute is at or after that day's closing time; an end of 12:00 AM adds no minutes to the next day. A booking with any minute in the next day is malformed (no `weekKey`). Phase F's settings must reject a closing time later than 12:00 AM. | Phase D plan (Codex review): the data model gave each booking one `weekKey` but never said what a Monday-crossing booking does. SPEC §4–5 already rule overnight bookings out. |
    74	| 57 | C7 | Sep 28 | `weekKey` is **ISO-8601**, with the ISO week-year and a two-digit week (`2026-W09`; 2027-01-01 → `2026-W53`). | At a year boundary, two readings of "week" give different keys. |
    75	| 58 | C8 | Sep 28 | A pause whose end is before its start is **malformed**, so `deriveStatus` gives `review` (rule 7). | Otherwise it would fall through to `active` silently; `review` is how #34 handles every other data gap. |
    76	| 59 | — | Sep 28 | A pause with a start date but **no end date** gives **`review`**, not "paused indefinitely" (rule 7 as written). The member can sign in and see the feed but can't book; staff see a flag and add the end date, and the pause's booking cancellation happens then. | Christie chose it over the alternatives. The D-5 implementation review found Membership Manager's Quick Log can save a pause with a blank end, so this is a reachable state, not a typo. |
    77	| 60 | D2 | Oct 1 | `functions/iam-expectations.json` allows **exactly one** Editor holder: `760301318440@cloudservices.gserviceaccount.com` (the Google APIs Service Agent). Editor held by anyone else, a build account above all, is still a difference. | Google granted it Editor during the first functions deploy (when the CLI turned on the Firebase Extensions API). It's Google-managed, nothing we deploy runs as it, and Google advises against removing its grant. Christie chose to allow it rather than remove it. |
    78	| 61 | D2 | Oct 1 | **IAM readings only when needed** (replaces "after every deploy"). The functions guard records `attest_needed` and why: needed for an unverified deploy, the first deploy of a codebase, a changed function set, declarations, IAM expectations, `firebase.json` or `.firebaserc`, a Google API turned on during the deploy, or a reading still owed. Otherwise "not needed", naming the attestation it relies on. | Christie asked why each deploy needed so many Console screenshots. The automatic checks (hash, settings, the unauthenticated probe) still run every time. A role changed by hand in the Console isn't seen, so take a reading after one. |
    79	| 62 | D-4 | Oct 1 | **Scheduled functions in the functions guard** (F13 amended for schedules only; plan `my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export`, V2/V3). A function is declared `"trigger": "https"` or `"trigger": "schedule"`; a declaration's keys are exact. A schedule declares its schedule, `timeZone` `"UTC"` and all five retry values, and no invoker (the CLI makes the runtime account its only caller); the guard and the backstop refuse a manifest whose values differ or are unset. The CLI can't read a Cloud Scheduler job, so Christie reads it in the Console and `--attest` checks it (`scheduler_attested`; the complete job list = every DEPLOYED schedule, i.e. each codebase's newest attempt). **Exception to "every HTTP response sets `x-tinker-reached`":** scheduled functions, whose response firebase-functions writes itself; the unauthenticated probe must still be refused. | M6 required the guard to read the schedule back before D-4's function ships; the CLI never clears a retry setting removed from code, and can't see a job at all. Christie chose the header exception (O6). |
    80	| 63 | D-4 | Oct 2 | **The weekly vault export** (plan `my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export`, V1, V4–V12, O5). A second functions codebase, `vault`, separate from the canary: `vaultExport` (scheduled, Sundays 09:00 UTC, 3 retries about 10/20/40 min apart, 30-min timeout) copies the whole database to its own folder `weekly/<UTC date>-<6 chars>/` in bucket **`my-clay-hub-vault-exports`** in `tinker-hq-vault` (US multi-region, 56-day lifecycle, 7-day soft delete, no versioning, no retention lock), and waits for Google to finish, every call held to the run's 1,500 s budget; it succeeds only when the export is done, error-free, SUCCESSFUL, at the expected prefix, and its folder is complete. A retry finds a complete folder ("already done") or the running export ("resume") before it starts anything; a rare duplicate is a second complete copy in its own folder, never a mixed one (O5). `vaultFresh` answers a Monitoring uptime check: 200 while the newest complete copy is at most 7 d 18 h old (the email lands at about 8 days). Accounts: `vault-export@` (custom role `vaultExporter`: export + operations get/list; `vaultLister` on the bucket), `vault-watch@` (`vaultLister` on the bucket only), the Firestore service agent (`firestoreExportWriter` on the bucket only, granted in V-4). No deployed function or service account can import into production (only Christie, as Owner, by hand: DATA-RESTORE.md). Built on `@google-cloud/firestore-api` 0.2.0 (where Firestore 9.3.0's admin client and protos live) rather than `@google-cloud/firestore` as the plan first named. Restoring: DATA-RESTORE.md. | The project's own backups (PITR, daily backups) live inside the project that could fail; this is the off-project copy that gates real member data (Phase E). Christie chose US, the uptime check and the 7 d 18 h threshold (O2, O4), a rare duplicate over a lock (O5), and a restore rehearsal after Phase E (O3). |
    81	| 64 | D-4 | Oct 7 | **A Force run is keyed by the day it actually runs.** V-4's failure-first Force run (Wed Oct 7) showed Cloud Scheduler sends a Force run the job's *next* scheduled time (Sunday Oct 11 09:00 UTC), not "now" as the V7 tests assumed, so a weekday catch-up run would have taken the coming Sunday's key and that Sunday's real run would have skipped as "already done". `runKey` now keys a scheduled time still in the future, or more than 12 h in the past (a Force run on a paused job might carry an old stored time; impl review, Claude), by today's UTC date; a real run and its retries (J15; all within about 3 h of the scheduled time) keep their scheduled date. Accepted limit (impl review, Codex): the header can't tell a Force run's retry from a new Force run, so a retry crossing UTC midnight is keyed by the new day and makes at most one extra complete copy in its own folder (O5), and possibly one extra failure email; if that day is Sunday, the copy is genuinely Sunday's and the 09:00 run's "already done" is correct. Runbook: start Force runs before 20:00 UTC. Until this ships: no Force run at all (on any day, including a Sunday after 09:00 UTC, it would take a later Sunday's key), and V-4's first real export is Sunday Oct 11's scheduled run. | Christie chose it (fix next, before Phase E; don't hold V-4) |
